      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lake Worth was built in 1914 as a Fort Worth reservoir and soon attracted North Texas visitors for boating and recreation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Worth_(Texas)",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Incorporated in 1939, Lake Worth evolved into a diverse residential and commercial hub while keeping its outdoor‑recreation roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://dallas.wiki/a/Lake_Worth_(City)",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The land around Lake Worth slopes downward toward the lake, shaping drainage patterns and influencing how the area is used.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://dallas.wiki/a/Lake_Worth_(City)",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mid‑2000s gas revenue from the Barnett Shale enabled Lake Worth to dredge the lake and plan a park comparable to Central Park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Worth_(Texas)",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In July 1969, residents reported a goat‑fish‑man creature, later dubbed the Lake Worth Monster, sparking one of Texas’s most famous cryptid tales.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
